# === Client fills these in ===

# Database password (generate a strong one, e.g.: openssl rand -hex 16)
PACGATE_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-strong-password

# --- Ollama residency (set automatically by install.ps1, listed for visibility) ---
#
# install.ps1 step 2b writes these at the USER level from a hardware tier, then
# restarts Ollama once. They are NOT read from this file - they belong to the
# Ollama process, not to the containers - so they are documented here only so an
# operator can see and question them. Values below are the AIPC-class tier
# (OS-visible RAM 24-55 GiB):
#
#   OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL      = 2
#   OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS = 2
#   OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE        = 15m
#   LLAMA_ARG_CACHE_RAM      = 1024      (MiB; AIPC-class tier)
#
# WHY, measured 2026-10-20 on Ollama 0.40.6:
#   - Nothing in this bundle used to set any OLLAMA_* variable, so every AIPC ran
#     stock defaults: NUM_PARALLEL=1 (one server slot per model) and the
#     machine's own context setting.
#   - With NUM_PARALLEL=1, three concurrent utility calls ran strictly serialised
#     (1.63s / 3.02s / 4.32s = 5.35s wall). At 2-3 the same calls overlap for
#     ~1 GB of extra KV. That serialisation is the "hangs with no generation"
#     symptom: a title call, a memory update and a summarizer all queue behind
#     the user's own chat on one slot.
#   - OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS is a CAP, not a target, and its default is already
#     3 per GPU. Leave it at 2 or higher: the binding limit is a memory-fit check
#     (the scheduler logs `system_limited=true`), so forcing 1 only makes
#     eviction unconditional. Verified: with MAX=1, loading gemma4 (7.6 GB)
#     evicted a resident ornith (5.6 GB) even with 100 GiB of GPU memory free.
#
# LLAMA_ARG_CACHE_RAM (added 2026-10-21) bounds llama.cpp's PROMPT CACHE, which is
# HOST RAM and is unrelated to VRAM. It defaults to 8192 MiB and had grown to
# 6.5 GiB on the HX370 box:
#
#   cache state: 4 prompts, 6506.381 MiB (limits: 8192.000 MiB, ...)
#
# which is enough to push that machine to 0.03 MB available memory during a
# workflow. The cache was also not paying for itself there: gemma4 is a
# sliding-window hybrid, and llama.cpp logs "forcing full prompt re-processing
# due to lack of cache data (likely due to SWA ...)" - measured reuse over a full
# day was only 22.8% of requested prompt tokens, with 48.5% of prefill events
# getting zero reuse. llama-server is a CHILD of ollama.exe, so it inherits this
# variable; no flag is passed for it. Verified: with the variable at 512 the
# runner's own log reported `limits: 512.000 MiB`.
#
# This is scoped to the Ollama process Pacgate starts, like the three above. It is
# NOT a host-wide setting.
#
# --- WSL memory ceiling: an OPERATOR decision, not applied by the installer ---
#
# On a machine with no %USERPROFILE%\.wslconfig, WSL2 takes a ceiling of half of
# physical RAM and does not promptly return freed guest page cache to Windows.
# Measured on the HX370 box: vmmemWSL held 30.38 GiB private while all containers
# together used 9.53 GiB, so ~21 GiB was charged to Windows that nothing was
# using. That is what a "50 of 64 GB used" reading actually is - commit charge,
# not working set.
#
# Pacgate does NOT write .wslconfig. That file is per-user and machine-wide: it
# governs every WSL distro, not only Docker, so changing it is the same boundary
# violation as rewriting Ollama's shared context setting. If this box pages
# during a workflow, the operator can bound it deliberately:
#
#   [wsl2]
#   memory=24GB
#   autoMemoryReclaim=gradual
#
# then `wsl --shutdown` (which stops every running container) and restart Docker.
# 24 GB is a ceiling, not an allocation, and leaves ~2.5x headroom over the ~9.5
# GiB the stack actually uses.
#
# CONTEXT IS NOT SET HERE. That is deliberate - see below.
#
# --- Model selection: standard Ollama tags, chosen per machine ---
#
# Ollama is SHARED infrastructure on these machines; other tools use the same
# server. So Pacgate does not rewrite the machine-wide context setting.
#
# Pacgate also does NOT build derived tags. An earlier revision ran `ollama create`
# to make `pacgate-mid` / `pacgate-low` with `num_ctx` baked in. That was removed on
# 2026-10-21 because it was wrong for a shipped product:
#
#   - Those tags exist in no ghcr image and not in origin/main, so they could not be
#     pre-pulled and every deployment diverged from the published artifact.
#   - They created a SECOND name for the same weights. Ollama keys residency by tag
#     name, so `pacgate-low` and `gemma4:12b-it-qat` (identical blobs) could not
#     co-reside. Measured on the HX370 box: 15.9 s and 15.4 s reloads when switching,
#     and 56 llama-server starts / 55 model loads / 13 evictions in one day. That
#     churn was the largest single latency source, and it was self-inflicted.
#   - They froze one context per tier instead of letting the machine decide.
#
# So the roster is exactly the standard tags `ollama-models.txt` pre-pulls, and any
# installed tag can be selected. Set these to whatever is installed on THIS machine:
#
#   PACGATE_MODEL_MID=ornith-1.5:9b        (6.6 GB)
#   PACGATE_MODEL_LOW=gemma4:12b-it-qat    (7.2 GB)
#
# The same tags are what the deer-flow model picker offers, so a local model chosen
# in the UI and a workflow tier chosen here are the same name - one resident copy.
#
# Context for every local model is whatever YOUR Ollama server is configured for.
# install.ps1 prints a per-hardware-tier recommendation and deliberately does not
# apply it: Ollama is shared with VS Code / Copilot and anything else you point at
# it, so a User-scope environment variable would change the whole machine, and the
# restart needed to make it take effect would drop live client sessions.
#
# To apply the recommendation without touching the shared server, start a second
# `ollama serve` on its own port with the variables scoped to that process, then
# point OLLAMA_BASE_URL at that port.
#
# The OpenAI-compatible endpoint's lack of per-request `num_ctx` is why a per-model
# tag was once used: verified that `options.num_ctx` and a top-level `num_ctx` on
# /v1/chat/completions are both IGNORED, and that OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH DOES apply
# to that endpoint.
#
# CAVEAT, measured on the HX370 box: the Ollama desktop app's own `context_length`
# (in %LOCALAPPDATA%\Ollama\db.sqlite) OVERRIDES OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH. The tier
# there says 32768 while `ollama ps` reports 65536. That app value is the operator's
# and is deliberately not touched, so treat the tier's number as a request, not a
# guarantee - read the effective value back with `ollama ps` before budgeting VRAM.
#
# HARDWARE-TIERING TRAP: the dev box is documented as "128 GB" but Windows only
# exposes 31.65 GiB of it, because the iGPU's fixed UMA carve-out (96 GiB, per
# registry qwMemorySize) is taken before the OS boots. So the dev box and an
# HX370 (32 GB) land in the SAME residency tier - the tier is keyed on
# OS-visible memory, not on DIMM labels.

# JWT secret (generate: openssl rand -hex 32)
PACGATE_JWT_SECRET=change-me-to-a-random-hex-string

# Self-registration gate for pacgate-api (POST /api/auth/register).
# compose.prod.yaml and compose.bundle.yaml set this to "false" for the client
# stack, which is what closes the exposure described in
# deploy/DEFECT-pacgate-api-open-registration.md. Listed here so the value is
# discoverable, and so an operator doing a supervised first install knows the
# knob exists: install.ps1 step 6a creates the FIRST admin through this route,
# so a supervised run may set it true and turn it back off afterwards.
# Only an explicit true/1/yes enables it; anything else (including unset) is
# treated as disabled by pacgate-api.
PACGATE_ALLOW_REGISTRATION=false

# One-time setup token for deer-flow's POST /api/v1/auth/initialize.
#
# /initialize creates the FIRST deer-flow admin, and it is necessarily PUBLIC —
# it cannot require a login, because before it runs there is no account to log
# in as. Without a token, whoever reaches the URL first becomes the admin. On a
# fresh AIPC that is a LAN-reachable URL, so this is the only thing standing
# between an unprovisioned machine and a stranger claiming it.
#
# Measured 2026-10-14 on the running stack: GET through nginx returns 405 (route
# exists and is routed), and the patch's own comment says that with neither
# variable set, "the endpoint stays public and the admin_count check is the only
# guard". Neither variable was set anywhere in this bundle.
#
# LEAVE THIS BLANK unless you want to choose the value. A blank value does NOT
# disable the gate: compose passes PACGATE_GENERATE_SETUP_TOKEN=1 whenever this
# is blank, so the gate is always armed and a per-process token is generated.
#
# To read the generated token (it changes on every deer-flow restart, so read it
# at the point you need it rather than caching it):
#   docker logs deer-flow 2>&1 | Select-String 'PACGATE SETUP TOKEN'
#
# To pin your own value instead, set it here (any string; quote it if it has
# spaces) and the generated one is not used.
PACGATE_SETUP_TOKEN=

# Tenant ID for this law firm (lowercase, no spaces)
PACGATE_TENANT_ID=default-firm

# === deer-flow + pacgate-mcp auth (register these users in Stage 3) ===
# deer-flow and pacgate-mcp authenticate with pacgate-api using these
# credentials. Register the same email/password via /api/auth/register in
# Stage 3 BEFORE the first tool call (pacgate-mcp authenticates lazily).
PACGATE_API_EMAIL=admin@pacgate-law.com
PACGATE_API_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-strong-password
# Optional: a pre-issued JWT token instead of email/password (leave blank to
# use email/password above).
PACGATE_JWT_TOKEN=
# Matter UUID that deer-flow's memory adapter scopes to (see handbook).
#
# DO NOT LEAVE THIS BLANK. deer-flow picks its memory storage by class path and
# wraps the instantiation in a bare except that falls back to its own
# FileMemoryStorage. A blank value here is not "unset": compose substitutes an
# empty string, the adapter raises on it, and deer-flow SILENTLY writes
# unsanitized memory prose to disk - outside all three matter-memory guards
# (the 422 scope rule, the 64 KiB cap, and the If-Match revision guard).
#
# scripts/test-memory-lane.ps1 fails the build if this is empty. Set it to the
# matter this deployment is scoped to, e.g. the UUID from `docker compose exec
# pacgate-api`'s seeded matter, or any matter the API reports on
# GET /api/matters.
PACGATE_MATTER_ID=00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000

# === Optional ===
# === Workflow model tiers ===
# Which models pacgate-api drives workflows with. Leave commented to accept the
# compose defaults.
#
# MEASURED 2026-10-18. The compose default for MAIN is gemma4:12b-it-qat (the local
# 12B), and that model CANNOT drive a document workflow: it calls list_documents and
# kb_search and then stops, never reaching generate_docx. The route still returns
# HTTP 200 and the run reports success, so it fails as an EMPTY RESULT rather than an
# error. Same test, only this variable changed:
#   MAIN=gemma4:12b-it-qat         -> list_documents, kb_search           (no docx) 60.7s -> 2 failed
#   MAIN=deepseek-v4.1-flash:cloud -> ..., generate_docx, read_document  (docx ok) 18.2s -> 19/19
#
# This matches the agreed architecture (2026-08-30): the cloud model does main
# prompting and end results; local models are UTILITY ONLY (title, memory,
# summarizer, VLM).
#
# DATA EGRESS: a cloud tag sends matter content to ollama.com. That is a deliberate
# posture decision for a legal deployment, so it is set per machine and can be
# commented out - at the cost of document workflows producing nothing on a 12B local
# model. Requires `ollama signin` on the machine (cloud tags have no local weights).
#
# LOW stays local: it is the utility tier and is not used for generation.
# MID is unused by the workflow library today (no workflow selects it), so it is left
# at the compose default. If you DO use it, note the default (qwen3.8:27b, 17 GB) does
# not fit a 16 GB AIPC - see scripts/test-aipc-capacity-fit.ps1.
#
# PACGATE_MODEL_MAIN=deepseek-v4.1-flash:cloud


# Set to true if using HTTPS in front of nginx (production with TLS)
PACGATE_COOKIE_SECURE=false

# Browser origins allowed to call deer-flow auth routes (login). The deer-flow
# gateway rejects cross-site auth POSTs (403 "Cross-site auth request denied")
# unless the Origin is allowlisted here. Default (when unset) is localhost only:
#   http://localhost:8089,http://localhost:8090
# Clients access the AIPC via IP/hostname (not localhost), so set this to the
# client's actual access origin, e.g.:
#   GATEWAY_CORS_ORIGINS=http://192.168.1.10:8089,http://localhost:8089,http://localhost:8090
# Comma-separated list of scheme://host[:port] origins.
GATEWAY_CORS_ORIGINS=

# === Data source connectors (optional) ===
# Uncomment and fill in only the connectors you need. Without keys, external
# legal search returns fewer sources; core functionality is unaffected.
# See deploy/AIPC-DEPLOYMENT-HANDBOOK.md "Known limitations".

# China-law search (regenerate token at https://mcp.pkulaw.com)
#PKULAW_API_KEY=

# US legal research
#VAQUILL_API_KEY=

# EU compliance MCP
#ANSVAR_API_KEY=

# Offshore corporate registry
#OPENCORPORATES_API_KEY=

# === OpenViking (memory lane) ===
# Root API key for the OpenViking server (generate: openssl rand -hex 32)
OPENVIKING_ROOT_API_KEY=change-me-to-a-random-hex-string
# API key clients use to call OpenViking (can equal root key in pilot)
OPENVIKING_API_KEY=change-me-to-a-random-hex-string

# Title generation must never freeze a run (silent-no-reply fix 2026-10-06):
# run c2a0ced4 (2026-10-06 12:12:55) stalled inside TitleMiddleware after an
# ollama /v1/chat/completions 200, showing no output until the user cancelled.
# An 8s fallback bound keeps the worst case cheap.
DEERFLOW_TITLE_INVOKE_TIMEOUT_S=8
